#edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510744 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110758 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186541137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110758 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110758 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186510753 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186110758 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110758 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1045 "Using the Pythagorean Identity in the First Quadrant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186110825 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ec05fcce-5a67-533c-840a-322806556dbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186107635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110987 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe2e1b6f-5340-5b4a-a389-958e36ca570f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110987 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{7}{8}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{49}{64}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{49}{64} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{64} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111735 #edb/ref 1061 87629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91d91920-7585-54d5-a3bb-aa4f02c97c17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111736 #edb/ref 1061 87630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "51014196-5277-5ae7-8d38-8cacaa66b4eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111737 #edb/ref 1061 87633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13bf9525-3d5e-5ef0-b342-b104b3a91ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111738 #edb/ref 1061 87634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5d4c5eb-0cce-5c47-afdd-6deeebc20fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111739 #edb/ref 1061 87637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7c20379-d544-52e6-9e90-0d619215daf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111741 #edb/ref 1061 87640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b539236-de75-501f-ac26-46341ff46dac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111867 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111867 #edb/ref 1065 2550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111867 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f0d65e8f-ee17-505a-8a0c-a8b8c87f02e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111867 #edb/ref 1067 "The Pythagorean Identity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111872 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111872 #edb/ref 1065 2718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111872 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b4029eb2-0222-5d8b-a285-7f0089655736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111872 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288050 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288050 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288050 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02a71e23-0acc-56b3-954d-83821eac7e61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288498 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{15}{32}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288498 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288498 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "44b4646f-0c19-57a3-bdf3-8ad3609e015a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288856 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7769db55-4f82-5ad4-abd8-f7f093ba576d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289280 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd830b0e-ef09-5fa8-8c16-55c6bc0ac78d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289500 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289500 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289500 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dc81901a-30cb-5088-a113-f77b69120c2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186289280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289946 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "64e92ee8-c463-5350-91b5-0bace844e68b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290449 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290449 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290449 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290449 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{1}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "122300d6-48f4-52e5-9b3e-da4687523cb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290449 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333108 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333108 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333108 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333119 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{55}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333155 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333155 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333155 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334183 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334183 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334183 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334192 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334369 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{56}{81}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334369 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334369 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334503 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334503 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334503 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334536 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{56}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af99c88e-1b1e-54e6-8e94-cc6dcf9705d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334926 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186334926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335143 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335313 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2831461c-74ea-58e4-8c19-3e42bd31b998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335575 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335735 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335735 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335735 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335735 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335735 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335783 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43fa1511-b2d2-52bf-92cb-53a58f4a78b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335783 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510724 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186110754.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186510724 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "4928ea21-58db-59e7-af0f-05e035ba58c8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186510727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510740 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186510727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186510727 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/ccdb5bb029197875aaeebfc899dff4d25597b657e64ea95d1b34d09df0a37789.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186510727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2014768a-a233-5eee-b9c5-afc2675420b0" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186510727 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/af/af413350ef3c9edde0eebf49cb8f37c8b20791f4bb01e04adaacaa0c378d28a1.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{43}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe 