c}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541091 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541091 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541091 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541091 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67eb7841-2530-5536-9fe7-7fe1cd1941b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541091 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541137 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ca/ca003331d04113119019996f15296ec2ab16fa0c14227a12f5f421c4c4103746.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d952daa8-27a5-56d3-8d17-8e3ce2fc3807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541137 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
